::: {.doc-subject}

List the v-structures of a directed graph: colliders whose causes are not adjacent.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L67940-L68038){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.v_structures()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![V-structures](../assets/graphs/v-structures.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Two colliders side by side. Left: talent and luck both point to success and are not joined to each other, an unshielded collider, drawn in the accent color. Right: x and y both point to z, but the edge x to y, left in full ink, links the two causes, so this collider is shielded and faded. The card returns the only v-structure, (talent, success, luck), and notes that (x, z, y) is shielded by x to y." width="100%"}

A v-structure (an *unshielded* collider) is a triple `p1 -> c <- p2` where `p1` and `p2`
are not joined by an edge. V-structures are what distinguishes Markov-equivalent causal
DAGs: two DAGs encode the same conditional independences exactly when they share their
skeleton and their v-structures (Verma and Pearl, 1990), so causal discovery algorithms
such as PC orient edges by finding them.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[tuple[int, int, int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   Every v-structure `(p1, c, p2)` as node IDs.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is undirected.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Filters `colliders()` for parent pairs with no edge in either direction. Matches
NetworkX's `dag.v_structures`.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~colliders`) 

:   All colliders, shielded or not.

[](`~moral_graph`) 

:   Join the parents of each node.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A collider is a v-structure only when its two causes aren't joined:

::: {#c3ce1fe9 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

open_v = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "C"), ("B", "C")], directed=True)
shielded = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "C"), ("B", "C"), ("A", "B")], directed=True
)

len(open_v.v_structures()), len(shielded.v_structures())
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(1, 0)
```
:::
:::


### Colliders that leave a mark

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. Of its 8 colliders, 6 are v-structures. The two others are
shielded: Age also affects Medication, and Genetics also affects Blood pressure.
V-structures matter because data can reveal them: they're what lets causal discovery
orient some arrows from observations alone:

::: {#7ef3989f .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal = ec.causal()
label = causal.get_label_from_id
shielded = sorted(set(causal.colliders()) - set(causal.v_structures()))

[[label(n) for n in triple] for triple in shielded]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
[['Age', 'Blood pressure', 'Medication'],
 ['Blood pressure', 'Heart disease', 'Genetics']]
```
:::
:::


The two shielded colliders are each a triangle, here in the accent color:

::: {#5238731e .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
causal.plot(
    layout="pos",
    highlight=[
        ("Age", "Blood pressure"), ("Medication", "Blood pressure"),
        ("Age", "Medication"), ("Blood pressure", "Heart disease"),
        ("Genetics", "Heart disease"), ("Genetics", "Blood pressure"),
    ],
    label_by="label", height=360,
)
```

